[[File:Tiong Bahru 11, Jul 06.JPG|thumb|A Singapore Improvement Trust corner block at Tiong Poh Road, Tiong Bahru — the estate that grew out of Hunter's 1925 recommendation.]]
'''Dr. Peter Sinclair Hunter''' (c. 1883/1884 – 28 March 1954) was the [[Municipal Commission of Singapore|Municipal]] Health Officer of Singapore from the mid-1920s until his retirement in 1939. He is best known for recommending, in 1925, the slum-clearance scheme that grew into the [[Tiong Bahru]] housing estate — a template later followed by the [[Housing and Development Board]] — and for leading Singapore's response to a serious malaria outbreak in the mid-1930s. He also contributed to a standard early study of malaria control in British Malaya.

==Early life==
Little has been documented of Hunter's life before he arrived in Singapore. He held the qualifications M.B., Ch.B. and a Diploma in Public Health (D.P.H.). Based on his stated age at death (70, in 1954), he was born around 1883 or 1884.

==Career==
Hunter joined the colonial health service of the [[Straits Settlements]] in May 1913.<ref name="retire">{{cite news |title=Dr. P. S. Hunter To Retire Next Friday |work=The Sunday Tribune |location=Singapore |date=5 March 1939}}</ref> His earliest work in the colony was hands-on rather than administrative: in those early years he served as a bacteriologist, called in to [[Middleton Hospital]] to attend post-mortem examinations.<ref name="middleton">{{cite news |title=Middleton Hospital Farewell To Dr. Hunter |work=[[The Straits Times]] |location=Singapore |date=8 March 1939}}</ref> In May 1914 the Governor commissioned him Surgeon-Lieutenant in the Medical Company of the [[Singapore Volunteer Corps]], the colony's part-time defence force.<ref>{{cite news |title=S.V.C. Orders in Brief |work=[[The Malaya Tribune]] |location=Singapore |date=13 May 1914}}</ref> A son was born to Dr. and Mrs. Hunter on 7 June 1915 at their home on Leonie Hill Road.<ref>{{cite news |title=Hunter (birth notice) |work=Pinang Gazette and Straits Chronicle |location=Singapore |date=10 June 1915}}</ref> In 1920 he was recorded as a member of the [[Straits Branch of the Royal Asiatic Society]], listed with a correspondence address care of the Crown Agents in London.<ref>{{cite journal |title=List of Members |journal=Journal of the Straits Branch of the Royal Asiatic Society |issue=85–86 |year=1922 |quote=1920. † Hunter. Dr. P. S., c/o Crown Agents. London.}}</ref>

By the mid-1920s Hunter held the post of Municipal Health Officer, responsible for the sanitary condition of the town under the [[Municipal Commission of Singapore|Singapore Municipal Commission]]. Overcrowding in [[Chinatown, Singapore|Chinatown]] troubled him enough to act on: in 1925 he recommended to the colonial government that a well-planned suburb be laid out near Chinatown specifically to relieve the pressure.<ref name="tbhp">{{cite news |title=Tiong Bahru Housing Plan |work=[[The Straits Times]] |location=Singapore |date=20 April 1935}}</ref> The [[Singapore Improvement Trust]] was established in 1927 to carry out large-scale planning and slum clearance, and the land at Tiong Bahru — recommended by Hunter — was purchased in 1926 and laid out under the Trust soon after.<ref name="tbhp"/> On 1 December 1936 the first block of Trust flats on Tiong Bahru Road was occupied, with eleven families moving in.<ref>{{cite news |title=Improvement Trust Flats Occupied |work=The Straits Budget |location=Singapore |date=10 December 1936}}</ref>

Housing was not Hunter's only responsibility. Singapore's anti-malaria campaign dated to 1911, when the colonial government brought in Dr. [[Malcolm Watson]] to apply the subsoil drainage methods he had used to make the malarial towns of [[Klang, Malaysia|Klang]] and [[Port Klang|Port Swettenham]] liveable; Hunter later contributed to Watson's standard published account of that campaign.<ref name="watsonbook">{{cite book |last=Watson |first=Malcolm |title=The Prevention of Malaria in the Federated Malay States: A Record of Twenty Years' Progress |publisher=John Murray |location=London |year=1921 |others=with contributions by P. S. Hunter and A. R. Wellington; preface by [[Ronald Ross]]}}</ref> By the 1930s, running and defending that campaign had become part of Hunter's own job as Municipal Health Officer. It became urgent in December 1935, when a mosquito known locally as the "ludlowi" was found breeding in large numbers around the [[Kallang]] Basin and malaria cases climbed with it; Hunter spent that month in the papers reassuring readers that the outbreak, while real, "does not represent a grave state of affairs," and warning days later that swift action was still needed to check it.<ref>{{cite news |title=Malaria Outbreak Not Dangerous — Dr. Hunter Gives Reassurance |work=[[The Malaya Tribune]] |location=Singapore |date=13 December 1935}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Swift Action Needed To Check Malaria |work=The Straits Budget |location=Singapore |date=19 December 1935}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Outbreak of Malaria |work=[[The Straits Times]] |location=Singapore |date=5 October 1935}}</ref> By the time he left for leave in England in May 1936, ''The Straits Times'' noted he had "had a busy time last year in supervising the campaign against malaria."<ref>{{cite news |title=Dr. Hunter's Leave |work=[[The Straits Times]] |location=Singapore |date=8 May 1936}}</ref> His own annual report that July, candid about not agreeing with everything the press had written about the epidemic, still thanked the Singapore newspapers for pushing the issue into public view and forcing action.<ref>{{cite news |title=Good Name of Singapore. Dr. Hunter's Report On Malaria |work=[[The Straits Times]] |location=Singapore |date=3 July 1936}}</ref>

Hunter was named in the [[1937 Coronation Honours]] list, alongside Municipal Commission president William Bartley, who received the [[Order of St Michael and St George|C.M.G.]] in the same list.<ref>{{cite news |title=Malayans in Coronation Honours List |work=[[Singapore Free Press|Singapore Free Press and Mercantile Advertiser]] |location=Singapore |date=11 May 1937}}</ref> By 1948, when he resurfaces in the papers as acting chairman of the [[Singapore Turf Club]], he is already styled [[Order of the British Empire|C.B.E.]]<ref>{{cite news |title=S'pore Turf Club To Resume Donations |work=The Straits Budget |location=Singapore |date=5 February 1948}}</ref>

==Retirement and later life==
Hunter retired on Friday, 10 March 1939, twenty-six years after he had arrived in the colony.<ref name="retire"/> His send-off was substantial: days before, he visited Middleton Hospital, where — the ''Straits Times'' reported — he had known every member of staff since the date of their employment, except the steward, who had joined before Hunter himself arrived.<ref name="middleton"/> A group photograph was taken to mark the visit, and colleagues gave him a farewell dinner at the home of a Mr. N. A. Sen.<ref>{{cite news |title=Farewell Dinner To Dr. P. S. Hunter |work=[[The Malaya Tribune]] |location=Singapore |date=8 March 1939}}</ref> In his own farewell interview with the ''Straits Times'', Hunter chose to talk about the problems still ahead rather than look back at what he had already done, prompting the paper's own editorial page to gently push back and remind readers what Singapore's health record had looked like before men like him got to work on it.<ref>{{cite news |title=Singapore Fever |work=The Straits Budget |location=Singapore |date=16 March 1939}}</ref>

Hunter remained in Singapore after retirement. By 1948 he was active enough in civic life to chair the Singapore Turf Club, telling its annual general meeting that the club's finances did not currently stretch to charitable donations.<ref>{{cite news |title=S'pore Turf Club To Resume Donations |work=The Straits Budget |location=Singapore |date=5 February 1948}}</ref> In March 1952, thirteen years after his retirement, his son — by then a solicitor in Surrey, and confusingly also styled "Mr. P. S. Hunter" — flew out from London with his wife to spend three weeks visiting his parents.<ref>{{cite news |title=Dr. Hunter's son here |work=[[The Straits Times]] |location=Singapore |date=9 March 1952}}</ref> Three separate Singapore newspapers ran the reunion with a photograph, the clearest surviving likeness of Hunter himself.<ref>{{cite news |title=Mr. P. S. Hunter, a Surrey solicitor... |work=The Straits Budget |location=Singapore |date=13 March 1952 |quote=photograph credited to The Straits Times}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Father and Son Meet |work=[[The Singapore Free Press]] |location=Singapore |date=10 March 1952}}</ref>

==Death==
Hunter died in [[Singapore General Hospital]] on 28 March 1954, aged 70.<ref name="obit">{{cite news |title=Death of Colony Housing Pioneer |work=The Straits Budget |location=Singapore |date=1 April 1954}}</ref> His obituary in ''The Straits Budget'' credited him with the two achievements that had defined his career: he had "played a major role in overcoming malaria in Singapore," and he "was a champion of better housing."<ref name="obit"/>

==Legacy==
Tiong Bahru remains the one piece of Hunter's legacy that is still widely visible today. His own office — the municipal health inspectorate responsible in one breath for housing, sanitation and vector control — is a direct institutional ancestor of Singapore's modern public-health apparatus, though the line between his 1925 recommendation and today's hawker-centre grading or mosquito-control programmes runs through many later reorganisations and cannot be drawn directly.

